Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. PETER'S APARTMENT - DAY

The apartment is lived-in, a little cluttered. MA (60s, frail, kind eyes) sits in an armchair, staring blankly at a GAME SHOW on a small television.

NICK (30s, burly, scars on his face, reddish-brown hair) enters, laden with six grocery bags, one in each hand. He moves with an intimidating presence. PETER (20s, observant, a little weary) follows, carrying a few lighter bags.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.
> He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls.
> When he walked in, people took notice.

Nick drops the bags on the kitchen counter with a THUD.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> We lived downtown in a big brick apartment building on Kirk Street.
> A fifth-floor walk-up, so it was a real pain in the ass to haul up the groceries.
> Luckily, when I went to the store with Nick, he could load up with about six of those shopping bags and take them up in one trip.

Peter watches Ma, who suddenly stands and shuffles, bumping into a side table.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Ma’s getting older and it’s hard for her to see.
> Sometimes she gets up in the middle of the night and runs into furniture.
> I don’t want her to fall down and nobody knows about it.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

PETER rides his bike, then walks it down the sidewalk towards the pizza place. He's about to enter when a skinny girl, SIONNA (20s, short brown hair, large backpack), trips on the uneven cement. She stumbles into Peter.

PETER
> Are you okay?

SIONNA
> Yes.

She brushes off her black V-neck shirt. A necklace with a fish symbol hangs around her neck. Her eyes are a striking, light blue.

PETER
> I work here, I’m just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
> Sionna.

PETER
> Sionna? That’s different. I never heard of that. What does that mean?

SIONNA
> My mom and dad named her after an Irish goddess, and then something about the River Shannon.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> I wasn’t really listening though, because she had the coolest eyes I ever saw.
> They were a real light blue color, and reminded me of the sky, like right before the sun comes up.

PETER
> Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
> No thanks, I don’t eat pizza, but if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on a scrap of paper and hands it to Peter. She walks away, looking back over her shoulder.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> It made me feel a little weird inside, like someone was lookin’ right through me or something.
> It was like I “knew” her or something. Like I had seen her somewhere before.

INT. PETER'S APARTMENT - DAY

Ma is in the kitchen, mumbling to herself, looking for something. Her pain pills are lined up on the counter.

PETER
> Ma, what’s the matter?

MA
> I can’t find my pain pills, I know I put them here somewhere. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

Peter stares at her.

PETER
> Ma, what are you talking about? We don’t go to church anymore.
> Your pills are right in front of you in the box for today.

Ma gives him a glassy, empty look. Then snaps out of it.

MA
> Oh, right, I am so sorry. I see them right here. I don’t know what got into me.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Wow, she was losing it. I would have to tell Nick about this.
> I’m sure he’ll get mad or something – like it was my fault.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Peter sits at the bar, watching a FOOTBALL GAME on a TV. Nick enters, slaps Peter on the back.

NICK
> Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

PETER
> I don’t know. Nothin’.

NICK
> What’s the matter? I can tell you’re hidin’ something. What’s goin’ on?

Peter tells him about Ma’s confusion. Nick’s face hardens.

NICK
> I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day.
> Why don’t you take her to the store or something?
> Do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

PETER
> Of course not. I just needed to get away from her for a while, that’s all.

Nick gives Peter a look that makes him flinch.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> God, sometimes I really hate Nick inside.
> I mean, he’s done so much for us, but at the same time, inside I really get tired of his shit.

Peter pays for his beer.

PETER
> I’ll watch it at home with her. I promise to get her out tomorrow and at least take a walk.

NICK
> That’s more like it.

Peter leaves.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter stands in a vast library, rows of books towering around him. He approaches a LIBRARIAN at a desk.

PETER
> How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN
> Do you have a library card?

PETER
> No, how do I get one of those?

She gives him a form. He fills it out, gets a temporary paper card.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> It was like I could get anything in here for free.
> Who needed school anyway when you could get the same thing for free in the library?

Peter wanders into the "classic literature" section. He picks out a book: "Wuthering Heights."

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> It was about a guy named Heathcliff and a girl named Catherine.
> So far, it was a little like a soap opera.
> I got the feeling that these two really loved each other. This reminded me of Sionna.

Peter checks out the book.

INT. THE JAVA HUT - DAY

Peter sits nervously at a small table. Sionna is already there, sipping coffee. Her light blue eyes are captivating.

PETER
> Hi.

SIONNA
> Of course I do. How are you doing?

PETER
> Fine, ummm, I was wondering if you want to go for that cup of coffee.

SIONNA
> Absolutely.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> She sounded so sure of herself, like she knew I was going to call at just that minute.
> When I looked into her eyes, I lost all track of time and forgot who I was and what I was doing.

PETER
> What are you reading right now?

PETER
> Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
> Yea, I know. I read it.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> What happened over the next half hour, though, made me think about the book in ways I never could have imagined.

SIONNA
> The love that Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.
> A love that never changes will destroy you.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> It reminded me of the way Nick is; strong and unwavering, but somehow it seemed dumb.
> I wondered if it would destroy him in the end too.

They smile at each other.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> We made another date to go to dinner on Friday night. I couldn’t wait.
> Maybe that was Ma’s problem all along. Dad left, and Ma never moved on. It just ended up destroying her.

INT. PETER'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma is in her armchair, clutching her chest. She looks distressed.

MA
> It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest! It’s so strange, the feeling I have right now. I’m a little dizzy too.

Peter looks worried. He picks up the phone.

PETER
> I’m calling Dr. Carson.

Dr. CARSON (O.S.)
> Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke.
> My advice is that you take her directly to the emergency room.

Peter hangs up, stunned.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> This was the last thing I expected, or hell, needed, today.
> That’s it, I was going to have to call Nick to leave work and pick us up.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma is asleep in a hospital bed. Peter dozes in a chair beside her.

DREAM SEQUENCE - EXT. BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

CHAOS. SCREAMS. BLOOD on the ground. PETER (in soldier's garb) stands over a fallen MAN.

DYING MAN
> Save her...

Blood bubbles from his mouth. Peter turns. NICK (in soldier's garb) runs towards him, a long knife in his hand.

NICK
> That you are little brother.

Nick plunges the knife into Peter's chest.

END DREAM SEQUENCE

Peter BOLTS UPRIGHT in the hospital chair, gasping for air, heart pounding. He looks at Ma, then around the room.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Why would Nick want to kill me? Me? His little brother.
> I felt betrayed and so confused. It took me a good fifteen minutes to come out of it.

INT. PETER'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter carefully places Ma's pills into a plastic container with days of the week labeled. Ma watches, looking overwhelmed.

PETER
> See Ma, right here is every day of the week, and every day will have the pills in you need.

MA
> Oh my Lord, that looks a little overwhelming.

PETER
> No worries Ma. I’m goin’ to be here to make sure everything is okay.

Ma looks at him with tired brown eyes.

MA
> You’re such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me. What would I do without you two?

Nick enters, his face beet red, hair disheveled.

NICK
> What’s up little Bro? You got Ma all tucked in? Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

Peter glares at him.

PETER
> Knock it off man. Somebody has to make sure she stays on track. Where have you been?

NICK
> Don’t start giving ME shit Bro. I was at work. More than you can say for your sorry ass down at that bullshit pizza parlor job.

Peter hands Nick the pill container.

PETER
> Look – I got her meds all in the same spot. Sionna helped me figure it out. She’s really smart.

Nick smirks.

NICK
> This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger. I mean, if you must know.

PETER
> Stop it Nick! You haven’t even met her. I don’t understand how you can’t like someone you don’t even know.

NICK
> Don’t you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

Nick stands, towering over Peter.

PETER
> I’m not a bastard!

NICK
> How the hell do you know? Maybe you are. You sure are a giant pussy – and that makes you and me way different Bro.

PETER
> You’re so Goddamn hateful. What the fuck got into you? Was Ma on crack or somethin’ when she was pregnant with you?

WHACK! Nick slaps Peter across the head. Peter's ear RINGS. He stumbles back.

NICK
> Don’t you EVER talk that way about MY mother you little fuck.

Nick spins and stomps out, slamming the door.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Christ, what just happened? I had never been in a screaming fight with Nick before.
> Me and Ma and Sionna would be just fine without him.
> In that split second, I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. SCHOOLYARD - DAY (FLASHBACK)

YOUNG PETER (9) and BOBBY (9) are doubled over, laughing hysterically. MRS. STARK (40s, stern, braids) approaches, glasses hanging from her necklace.

MRS. STARK
> Mr. Olsen! Mr. Olsen! Would you like to explain yourself to me before I send you in to talk to Mr. Pauls?

YOUNG NICK (11, taller, confident) walks up.

YOUNG NICK
> Um, sorry Mrs. Stark. He didn’t mean anything by it. He and Bobby were just goofing around.
> I’ll talk to him and Ma will talk to him. He’ll never act up in class again. I guarantee it.

Mrs. Stark puts on her glasses, sizes up Nick.

MRS. STARK
> Well, Nick, I know you’re a good student. I guess I’ll let it pass this time.
> YOU, young man, need to follow your brother’s example and start paying attention.

She turns and marches away.

YOUNG PETER
> Nick, um thanks man. You saved my skin.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> It made me love him. It made me hate him.
> It was like Nick lived in a bubble, and no one could touch him.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Peter is making pizzas. He looks out the window at the street.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> A couple months had gone by since my fight with Nick. He was now avoiding us like the plague.
> Since I had started meditation, things weren’t so critical.
> I could “see” my mind like I was watching a movie.

KEVIN MCCLEARY (30s, muscular, gelled hair) enters, excited.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
> Man, I heard what happened at Joe’s. What the hell?
> I heard you stopped Nick from beating some guy’s ass! Man! That’s tough shit!

Peter's eyes widen in alarm.

PETER
> Look, I don’t know who told you that, but it totally isn’t the truth.
> Nick almost kicked my ass, so don’t think I’m a hero or anything.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
> Man, you were right in Nick’s face. You moved like Bruce Lee, dog!
> Everybody is talking about it. Nick’s little brother is a badass!

Peter forces a smile.

PETER
> Don’t believe everything you hear.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> What is going on around here? How do stories get so confused?
> Most of the time, I would be glad to be the hero of any story, just not one that involved Nick.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Nick, BOB (30s, flannel shirt, beer belly), and SAMMY (30s) are at the bar. Nick points to the pool tables.

NICK
> That looks like Lindsey over there. Who’s she with? Who’s that asshole?

Peter looks at Nick, annoyed.

PETER
> Why does it matter Bro? You broke up with her – remember?

NICK
> I just keep tabs on her. You know what I mean? No one told me she was seeing anyone.

Nick's face contorts with rage. He walks towards LINDSEY (30s, blonde) and MARCO (30s, dark wavy hair, protective stance). Bob and Sammy follow. Peter reluctantly follows.

NICK
> What’s up Lindsey.

LINDSEY
> Oh, hi Nick. Not much.

Marco puts his arm around Lindsey. His eyes narrow.

MARCO
> Who is this?

LINDSEY
> Oh, just an old friend.

NICK
> Where did you meet this one? I didn’t hear of you dating anybody.

PETER
> Nick – leave it alone.

Nick shoves Peter aside.

MARCO
> What the fuck is wrong with you? You got a problem with me?

Nick's nostrils flare. His fists clench. Bob and Sammy look ready for a fight. AC/DC music BLARING.

PETER
> Nick, Nick, listen – you don’t have any problem with this guy.
> Leave it man, just leave it.

NICK
> Keep out of it! It’s none of your Goddamn business.
> You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face asshole.

Lindsey flattens herself against the wall.

PETER
> Stop it! Stop it! You’re crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego Nick!

Nick's face twists. His right fist slowly draws back towards Peter. Time seems to slow.

JOE (O.S.)
> HEY!

Joe and a BOUNCER rush in. They grab Nick. Nick head-butts the Bouncer. Sammy and Bob yell. Lindsey pulls Marco towards the door.

Peter turns and RUNS.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter bursts through the door, chest heaving, face pale. Sionna rushes to him.

PETER
> He tried to hit me. He tried to kill that guy. I ran.

SIONNA
> Your brother tried to hit you. No one does that, you know. I mean, no one who really cares about you.

PETER
> He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
> He is like a thorn in your side. He obviously has issues with his self-esteem.
> You are the voice of reason, and he doesn’t understand reason.

Peter looks at her, a new understanding dawning. Sionna strokes his face.

SIONNA
> You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can’t let him intimidate you.

Peter rests his head in her lap.

DREAM SEQUENCE - EXT. LAKESIDE - NIGHT

The world is black and white. PETER, exhausted, in dirty tunic, walks towards a lake. He cups water to drink. The wind HOWLS.

From the middle of the lake, SIONNA rises, an angelic figure in white fabric, swirling with wind and water. She directs nature.

The world bursts into COLOR. Peter holds out his arms. Sionna walks effortlessly through the water. They embrace. Serenity.

Suddenly, ALARM. NICK emerges from the woods, a giant with fire eyes, whirling a mace. He advances.

Peter steps in front of Sionna. She spreads her arms. An invisible force pushes Nick back. Nick shrinks, disappears.

Sionna smiles. Peter strokes her hair, kisses her. Her lips are real.

END DREAM SEQUENCE

Peter BOLTS UPRIGHT, almost hitting Sionna.

PETER
> What the? I had the craziest dream. You were an angel, and came out of the water.
> You protected us from Nick with some kind of magical force!

Sionna giggles, sleepy.

SIONNA
> Well, that sounds about right. Let’s go to bed and get some sleep.

INT. PETER'S APARTMENT - MORNING

Peter enters the quiet apartment. He finds it empty. He walks to the phone. The red message light BLINKS. He presses the button.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
> Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department.
> We need to talk to you regarding your family.

NURSE OWENS (V.O.)
> Hello Mr. Olson, this is Patricia Owens, one of the nurses at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

Peter's face drains of color. He rewinds, copies the number, and calls.

PETER
> Yeah, I guess. What's going on? Is Ma okay? I mean, I wasn't here last night.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
> We just think it best if you came in to talk directly to us.

Peter hangs up, frantic.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Peter sits opposite OFFICER DUPUIS (50s, graying hair, thin mustache). Peter's hands shake, holding a styrofoam cup of water.

OFFICER DUPUIS
> Mr. Olson. There has been a serious accident involving your brother, Mr. Nicholas Olson, and your mother, a Mrs. Olivia Olson.
> Last night about 8 p.m. they were hit broadside.
> I regret to tell you that your brother is in serious condition at Frodo and your mother passed away at the scene.

Peter stares, stunned. The cup rattles in his hand.

OFFICER DUPUIS
> Her body is at Sacred Heart Funeral Home. I know this is a huge shock to you.

Peter can only manage a whisper.

PETER
> Nick?

OFFICER DUPUIS
> Toxicology was pending because we think Nick may have possibly been drunk.

Peter's eyes close in despair.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Peter stands outside Nick's room. He sees Nick through the window: bruised, tubes, erratic hair. Peter enters.

Nick lies in bed, pale, a chest tube visible. He flicks off the TV.

NICK
> Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up, man. Can’t believe it. Can’t believe Ma’s gone.

Tears well in Nick's bloodshot eyes.

PETER
> They took care of it. The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK
> I didn’t mean to have 3 Jack and Cokes before he remembered he needed to take her to church.
> The cops are after me now. They’re telling me it could be vehicular manslaughter.

PETER
> Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick looks furious. Peter realizes Nick is powerless.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> All of his bullying, all of his violence, it meant nothing at this moment.
> He was trapped in a hospital bed with tubes sticking out of him, and he couldn’t hurt me.

PETER
> Think about it, Nick.

Peter turns and leaves.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> I no longer gave two shits about Nick. I lost my mom, and it seemed I had also lost my brother.

INT. FUNERAL HOME - DAY

Peter and Sionna sit with a FUNERAL DIRECTOR. Peter looks hollow. Sionna is calm, supportive.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> How do you want to package up that body? Do you want the top of the line casket with engraved flowers on the lid?
> The whole thing was so bizarre. One day you're talking to her, the next day you're putting her in a box.

Sionna speaks softly to the director, making decisions.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Sionna was the voice of reason in a lot of these decisions.
> I realized that I would be the only son there, as the oldest one would not be able to attend his own mother's funeral.
> Rightly so, since he killed her.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - DAY (MONTHS LATER)

Sionna sits on the couch, withdrawn, staring at the floor. Her arm is in a sling. Peter tries to engage her.

PETER
> Sionna? What’s going on? Are you depressed? What are you thinking?

SIONNA
> I don’t know. It’s hard to figure out exactly how I’m feeling. I guess I’m a little depressed.

PETER
> You know I love you, right? God damn it, look at me!
> I feel like you’re blaming me for this whole thing. You know it had nothing to do with me, right?

Sionna's light blue eyes well with tears.

SIONNA
> I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

Peter squats down, pleading.

PETER
> Please know that I love you. You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
> You say that, but deep inside I still have my doubts.
> As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you.
> Maybe we just need a break so I can hash things out with a therapist.

Peter's eyes betray him. He's desperate.

PETER
> Sionna, don’t do that. Stay here with me and we can work things out. Please?

Sionna gives a weak, hopeless smile. She slowly walks into the bedroom.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Sionna was my life guide. She was my personal savior in a way.
> She had awoken me from a monotonous life and taken me on a journey to a new, more exciting one.
> Now I had no one. My exit from the house was a free fall into the black, endless void of space.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY (SEVEN YEARS LATER)

PETER (now 30s, well-dressed, confident) walks down a busy Seattle street. The sky is a thick gray blanket.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Many years had passed since my Milwaukee days. Years of pain and growth, years of regret and loss.
> Thanks to my old boss, Tony, I had done pretty well for myself in the pizza business.
> I now had my own chain of pizzerias here in the Northwest. Tony's Boy's Pizza.

Peter passes a coffee shop, sees CHRIS (30s, barista, blonde hair in a bun). He hesitates, then walks on.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> I haven't really dated since Sionna left my life.
> How many years has it been now? Seven years since my life suddenly stopped being what it was.
> Seven years of change, seven years of loneliness, and seven years of hatred for my brother.

INT. DELI - DAY

Peter sits at a table, eating a Reuben. BOB (40s, graying, heavier) enters, spots Peter.

BOB
> Holy shit. Is that you, bro?

Bob cackles, slams his burger down, rushes to Peter, slaps his back.

BOB
> Wow, man, you look so grown up. Big shoulders and arm muscles too, man.

PETER
> Bob, what the hell is going on? Imagine seeing you here.

BOB
> You mean to visit Nick, right? He looks sober than I had ever seen before.

PETER
> Yeah, that’s right. I haven’t seen him for 8 years. I thought it was time.

BOB
> Wow, dude. You have some cojones now. I don’t think I could forgive him myself.

PETER
> Well, that’s kind of why I’m here. I need to talk things out with him. I need to let the shit go.

BOB
> I haven’t seen Nick for a couple of years. It was such a downer, man. He looks so sad and guilty.

PETER
> Bob, it’s not your responsibility to look after Nick. He made his own choices. You have to move on with your life too.

Bob looks at Peter, a new respect in his eyes.

BOB
> You are a wise man. You’re so right, man. I’m working at the factory now, making some pretty good dough. I took over his apartment.

They hug.

BOB
> I’m so proud of you, man. You got out of here and did something with yourself after all that shit.

EXT. SIONNA'S OLD APARTMENT - DAY

Peter drives up to Sionna's old duplex. It looks smaller, grayer, neglected. The porch is bare.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> It made me sad to see it. As if the house had a soul and felt lonely without us.

FLASHBACK - EXT. SIONNA'S PORCH - DAY

PETER and SIONNA sit on bamboo rockers. A cat, SMILEY, winds around Peter's legs. Peter almost rocks on her tail. Smiley SQUEALS and runs.

PETER
> I love Smiley because I love you. There’s nothing I wouldn’t do for you, babe.

SIONNA
> I love you too.

She gives him an air kiss, returns to her paper.

END FLASHBACK

Peter stares at the porch screen.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Every wish inside me, every longing to get back to her, was countered by the logic I could see from her side.
> I loved, I love her so much. I just want her to be happy.
> I am doing the right thing by leaving her alone and letting her and me move on with life.

Peter starts the car, drives away.

INT. CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

Peter sits in a sterile visitation room. A GUARD explains the rules. Nick enters, looking much older, gaunt, graying hair, sad eyes. He sits opposite Peter, separated by thick plexiglass.

NICK
> Hi, bro. Glad you came.

PETER
> Hi, Nick. Good to see you finally. How are you holding up?

NICK
> It’s not a cakewalk, but I got my routine down. It’s looking pretty good for getting out early. Maybe as early as 2 more years.

Peter takes a deep breath.

PETER
> I’m here to tell you that even though you are my brother and we have been through a lot of shit together,
> I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me.
> You’re an asshole that killed my mother in a drunk driving accident.
> You’re an asshole that held me down my entire life.
> But mostly, you’re an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life, which drove her away.
> I’ll never see you again because of you, Nick. Because you’re an asshole.

Nick looks down, silent. Peter's stomach muscles relax.

PETER
> I forgive you. I forgive you for being a huge asshole because it sets me free.
> It sets me free to never have anger or regret or feelings of loss.
> I forgive you, Nick, because it also releases your control over me forever, and that’s what I need.

Nick looks up, tears streaming down his face.

NICK
> I’m so sorry. You’re so right, man. I did do all that shit.
> I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Siona. It’s all I think about.
> You’re more than I ever was, you know. You’re a big man, and I’m a small one.

The Guard approaches.

GUARD
> Time’s almost up.

PETER
> After all is said and done, I want you to get better. I still believe in you.
> I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK
> I hear you, man. I love you too. Say, what are you doing now?

PETER
> I own a pizza chain in Seattle and we’ll be expanding. You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick. I really did it.

Nick's eyes widen, filled with pride.

NICK
> Wow, man. President Olson. That’s fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

The Guard signals. Nick smiles weakly.

NICK
> Bye.

Peter smiles back, hangs up the phone. He watches Nick being shuffled away.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> I had finished my quest and felt completely exonerated.
> That part of my life was closed now, and it was a complete relief.

EXT. WISCONSIN COUNTRYSIDE - DAY

Peter drives through rolling green hills. The sun shines.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> Time whizzing past me at an unbelievable pace.
> Once, my life seemed stagnant and dull. Now it’s filled with possibility and challenges.
> I had to think that Sionna was the catalyst for this. She dug me up and planted me, so to speak.

INT. AIRPORT - DAY

Peter sits in a boarding area, a bag of nuts and tea beside him. He looks calm, at peace.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> The weight of 30 years was gone, and I was free to live without fear, guilt, sadness, or judgment.
> I was sending Nick love and hope. I wish him the best, but I know I will never see him again.

An ATTENDANT scans Peter's boarding pass. Peter looks down at his name.

<center>NARRATOR (V.O.)</center>
> His voice entered my head again, and I could see his eyes filled with tears.
> I could hear his voice, filled with pride.

PETER (V.O.)
> President Olson. That’s me. President Peter Olson.

FADE OUT.
THE END.
